\chapter*{Unit 2B Hub: Applications of Derivatives}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Unit 2B Hub: Applications of Derivatives}

Unit 2A taught how derivatives are defined and computed. Unit 2B turns those calculations into information and decisions. The central question is no longer merely ``what is the derivative?'' but ``what does this derivative let us conclude?''

\begin{bgtranslation}[title={The practical meaning}]
A derivative is useful when its sign, size, units, or zeros answer a real question. Positive or negative may describe direction. A zero may identify a candidate peak. A second derivative may show whether growth is speeding up or slowing down. A tangent line may replace a difficult function near one convenient input.
\end{bgtranslation}

\subsection*{Main sequence}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Interpretation, higher derivatives, motion, and rates from data.
\item Local linearity, differentials, uncertainty, and Newton's method.
\item Related rates and geometric modeling.
\item Extrema, the Mean Value Theorem, increasing/decreasing behavior, concavity, and curve analysis.
\item Optimization in geometry, science, engineering, and business.
\item L'Hopital's Rule and indeterminate forms.
\item Modeling studios, advanced notes, cumulative review, and exams.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{bgsource}
The application-first organization was informed by the modern activities in \emph{Active Calculus}; the physical intuition in \emph{Calculus Made Easy}; and the public-domain applied traditions in Greenhill and Granville--Smith. BetterGrades prose, problems, models, checks, and digital specifications are independently written unless explicitly attributed.
\end{bgsource}
