\section{Compare the Signs of Velocity and Acceleration}
\lessonobjective{Determine when a particle speeds up or slows down.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Before the formulas}]
The graphs in \emph{Speeding Up and Slowing Down} should be read vertically through a common input. A height on the derivative graph is a slope on the original graph. A zero of velocity is a horizontal tangent of position; a zero of acceleration is a horizontal tangent of velocity.

Do not match graphs by superficial shape. Translate one feature at a time: sign, zeros, increasing behavior, and concavity. This produces a defensible interpretation even when the graphs are unfamiliar or not drawn to a convenient scale.
\end{bgexposition}

\BGVTwoBSpeedSigns




\begin{bgexposition}[title={Speed changes according to whether velocity and acceleration cooperate}]
Speed is \(|v|\), so it increases when acceleration pushes velocity farther from zero. That occurs when velocity and acceleration have the same sign. Speed decreases when acceleration pushes velocity toward zero, which occurs when their signs differ.

A sign chart is safer than verbal intuition here. Negative velocity means motion in the negative direction, not automatically slowing down. An object with \(v<0\) and \(a<0\) is moving left faster and faster.
\end{bgexposition}

Speed is the magnitude \(|v|\), so an object speeds up when velocity moves away from zero and slows down when velocity moves toward zero. The clean sign test follows: velocity and acceleration with the same sign imply speeding up; opposite signs imply slowing down.

This is easier to understand on a number line than through memorization. Acceleration points in the direction that velocity is being pushed. If that push reinforces the current direction, speed grows; if it opposes the current direction, speed shrinks.

\begin{bgmethod}[title={Sign test for speed}]
\begin{itemize}
\item If \(v\) and \(a\) are both positive, the particle moves forward faster.
\item If \(v\) and \(a\) are both negative, the particle moves backward faster.
\item If their signs differ, the particle slows down.
\end{itemize}
\end{bgmethod}

\begin{bgguided}[title={Use a combined sign chart}]
For the previous motion
\[
v(t)=3(t-1)(t-3),\qquad a(t)=6(t-2),
\]
determine speeding-up and slowing-down intervals on \((0,5)\).
\begin{bgsolution}
The critical times for signs are \(1,2,3\). Test the four intervals:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|cccc}
interval&\((0,1)\)&\((1,2)\)&\((2,3)\)&\((3,5)\)\\\hline
\(v\)&+&-&-&+\\
\(a\)&-&-&+&+\\
behavior&slow&speed&slow&speed
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Thus the particle speeds up on \((1,2)\cup(3,5)\) and slows down on \((0,1)\cup(2,3)\).
\end{bgsolution}
\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgcheck}{motion-sign-01}{multiple_choice}{speeding-up}[Quick check]
At an instant, \(v=-4\) m/s and \(a=-2\) m/s\(^2\). Is the object speeding up or slowing down?
\begin{bgreveal}Both quantities are negative, so the magnitude of velocity is increasing. The object is \(\boxed{\text{speeding up}}\).\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}
