\section{Translate Shape into Derivative Information}
\lessonobjective{Infer signs and trends of derivatives from graphs, and reconstruct plausible original functions from derivative graphs.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Before the formulas}]
The graphs in \emph{Reading Function and Derivative Graphs} should be read vertically through a common input. A height on the derivative graph is a slope on the original graph. A zero of velocity is a horizontal tangent of position; a zero of acceleration is a horizontal tangent of velocity.

Do not match graphs by superficial shape. Translate one feature at a time: sign, zeros, increasing behavior, and concavity. This produces a defensible interpretation even when the graphs are unfamiliar or not drawn to a convenient scale.
\end{bgexposition}




\begin{bgexposition}[title={Translate heights on one graph into slopes on another}]
To sketch \(f'\) from \(f\), begin where the slope information is clearest. Horizontal tangents on \(f\) become zeros of \(f'\). Rising intervals become positive regions; falling intervals become negative regions. Steeper parts of \(f\) produce larger magnitudes on \(f'\).

Do not copy the shape of \(f\). The derivative graph records slope, not height. A high point on \(f\) can correspond to a zero on \(f'\), and a low point on \(f\) can do the same.
\end{bgexposition}

Graph translation is a language skill. A rising original graph corresponds to a positive derivative; a local maximum corresponds to a derivative that changes from positive to negative; and a straight segment corresponds to a constant derivative. The derivative graph should be constructed from slopes, not from the heights of the original graph.

Read from both directions. Given \(f\), infer \(f'\); given \(f'\), reconstruct possible behavior of \(f\). The second task is less unique because many original functions can share the same derivative up to a vertical shift.

For a graph of \(f\):
\begin{itemize}
\item \(f'>0\) where \(f\) increases;
\item \(f'<0\) where \(f\) decreases;
\item \(f'=0\) at horizontal tangents;
\item large \(|f'|\) means a steep graph;
\item \(f''>0\) where slopes are increasing;
\item \(f''<0\) where slopes are decreasing.
\end{itemize}

\begin{bgguided}[title={Read a derivative graph as motion}]
Suppose \(s'(t)=v(t)\) is positive on \((0,2)\), zero at \(2\), negative on \((2,5)\), and increasing throughout. Describe \(s\).
\begin{bgsolution}
Because \(v>0\), position increases on \((0,2)\). Because \(v<0\), position decreases on \((2,5)\). The particle changes direction at \(t=2\). Since \(v\) is increasing, \(s''=v'>0\), so the position graph is concave up throughout. A concave-up graph can still decrease; it simply decreases with slopes that become less negative.
\end{bgsolution}
\end{bgguided}

