\section{Use the Sign of \(f'\)}
\lessonobjective{Determine intervals of increase and decrease from a derivative sign chart.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Before the formulas}]
In \emph{Increasing and Decreasing Intervals}, derivatives become evidence about shape. A zero or undefined derivative creates a candidate, a sign chart determines rising and falling behavior, and the second derivative describes how slopes change. No single fact tells the entire graph story.

State intervals, not just isolated points. Explain why a sign has the consequence you claim. When a theorem is used, verify its hypotheses explicitly; continuity and differentiability are not ceremonial phrases but the conditions that make the conclusion reliable.
\end{bgexposition}




\begin{bgexposition}[title={The sign of \(f'\) controls direction}]
A positive derivative means nearby outputs rise as the input increases. A negative derivative means they fall. Critical numbers and points where \(f'\) is undefined divide the domain into intervals on which the sign can be tested.

Use the derivative's sign, not the sign of the function. A function can be below the axis and increasing, or above the axis and decreasing.
\end{bgexposition}

The sign of \(f'\) converts local slope information into interval behavior. Positive derivative means the function rises as \(x\) increases; negative derivative means it falls. A sign chart organizes this information around critical numbers and domain breaks.

Test intervals, not isolated points. One sample point works only because the derivative's sign is known not to change inside the interval under consideration.

If \(f'(x)>0\) on an interval, \(f\) is increasing there. If \(f'(x)<0\), \(f\) is decreasing.

\begin{bgmethod}[title={First-derivative sign chart}]
\begin{enumerate}
\item Find critical numbers and domain breaks.
\item Place them on a number line.
\item Determine the sign of \(f'\) on each interval.
\item Translate positive signs to increasing and negative signs to decreasing.
\end{enumerate}
\end{bgmethod}

\begin{bgguided}[title={Analyze a cubic}]
For
\[
f(x)=x^3-3x^2-9x+1,
\]
find intervals of increase and decrease.
\begin{bgsolution}
We already found
\[
f'(x)=3(x-3)(x+1).
\]
Critical numbers are \(-1\) and \(3\). Test signs:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|ccc}
interval&\(( -\infty,-1)\)&\((-1,3)\)&\((3,\infty)\)\\\hline
sign of \(f'\)&+&-&+
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Therefore \(f\) increases on
\[
\boxed{(-\infty,-1)\cup(3,\infty)}
\]
and decreases on
\[
\boxed{(-1,3)}.
\]
\end{bgsolution}
\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgcheck}{increase-01}{interval}{(-infinity,0) union (2,infinity)}[Quick check]
For \(f'(x)=x(x-2)\), where is \(f\) increasing?
\begin{bgreveal}The product is positive outside the roots, so \(\boxed{(-\infty,0)\cup(2,\infty)}\).\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}
