\section{Classify Local Extrema by Sign Changes}
\lessonobjective{Use changes in the sign of \(f'\) to classify local extrema.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Before the formulas}]
Graph analysis in \emph{The First Derivative Test} is a coherent reconstruction problem. Domain, intercepts, limits, derivative signs, critical points, concavity, and asymptotes constrain the same curve. Build the picture in layers rather than trying to sketch from the original formula at once.

Keep a feature table. Each row should state the calculation, the interval or point, and the graphical consequence. This makes the final sketch a summary of evidence instead of an artistic guess.
\end{bgexposition}

\BGVTwoBSignChart




\begin{bgexposition}[title={A turning point is detected by a sign change}]
At a local maximum, the function changes from increasing to decreasing, so \(f'\) changes from positive to negative. At a local minimum, \(f'\) changes from negative to positive. If the sign does not change, the critical point is not a local extremum.

This test describes behavior on both sides and works even when \(f'\) is undefined at the critical point, provided the original function is defined there.
\end{bgexposition}

The first derivative test classifies a critical point by observing what the function does on either side. A change from increasing to decreasing produces a local maximum; decreasing to increasing produces a local minimum; no sign change produces neither.

This test handles cases where \(f''\) is zero or undefined, so it is often more robust than the second derivative test. It also explains the result through actual motion of the graph rather than a memorized sign table.

\begin{bgtheorem}[title={First Derivative Test}]
At a critical number \(c\):
\begin{itemize}
\item if \(f'\) changes from positive to negative, \(f\) has a local maximum;
\item if \(f'\) changes from negative to positive, \(f\) has a local minimum;
\item if \(f'\) does not change sign, there is no local extremum.
\end{itemize}
\end{bgtheorem}

For the previous cubic, \(f'\) changes \(+\to-\) at \(-1\), so \(f\) has a local maximum there. It changes \(-\to+\) at \(3\), so \(f\) has a local minimum there.

\begin{bgguided}[title={A critical point that is not an extremum}]
Classify the critical point of \(f(x)=x^3\) at \(0\).
\begin{bgsolution}
\[
f'(x)=3x^2.
\]
The derivative is zero at \(0\), but positive on both sides. The function is increasing before and after \(0\), so there is no local extremum. The horizontal tangent is an inflection point.
\end{bgsolution}
\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgmistake}
Solving \(f'(x)=0\) finds candidates. It does not classify them. A zero derivative may be a maximum, minimum, flat inflection point, or part of a constant interval.
\end{bgmistake}


\begin{bgmodelinglab}[title={Peak concentration after a dose}]
Suppose
\[
C(t)=20te^{-0.5t}
\]
models drug concentration for \(t\ge0\). Then
\[
C'(t)=20e^{-0.5t}(1-0.5t).
\]
The exponential factor is positive, so the derivative changes sign when \(1-0.5t=0\), at \(t=2\). Concentration increases before \(2\) hours and decreases after, so the first derivative test identifies a peak at \(t=2\).
\end{bgmodelinglab}
