\begin{bgbridge}[title={Keep the equality attached to limits}]
The safe notation is $\lim f/g=\lim f'/g'$ when the theorem applies. Writing $f/g=f'/g'$ is generally false. Keeping the limit symbols visible prevents a theorem about nearby behavior from being mistaken for an identity between functions.
\end{bgbridge}
\section{A Limit Theorem with a Narrow Entrance}
\lessonobjective{State why the rule requires an indeterminate form and why \(f/g\) is not generally equal to \(f'/g'\).}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Before the formulas}]
The central discipline in \emph{L'Hopital's Rule Is Not Quotient Cancellation} is form identification. L'Hopital's Rule is available only for a quotient whose numerator and denominator limits produce $0/0$ or $\infty/\infty$, together with the theorem's other conditions. A complicated fraction is not automatically eligible.

Substitute first and write the form explicitly. If the form is eligible, differentiate numerator and denominator separately and re-evaluate the new limit. If it is not, use algebra, a trigonometric identity, comparison, or a transformation that creates an eligible quotient.
\end{bgexposition}



\begin{bgexposition}[title={Differentiate the top and bottom; do not use the quotient rule}]
L'Hopital's Rule replaces \(f/g\) by the limit of \(f'/g'\). It does not compute the derivative of the quotient. Using the quotient rule would answer a different question.

The equality is between limits under hypotheses, not between the original functions. Writing the limit symbol on every line helps preserve that distinction.
\end{bgexposition}

L'Hopital's Rule compares the limiting behavior of two changing quantities through their derivative ratio. It does not simplify a quotient as an algebraic identity. Away from the limit, the functions \(f/g\) and \(f'/g'\) can be completely different.

Before applying the rule, substitute into the original limit. Only the indeterminate quotient forms \(0/0\) and \(\infty/\infty\) enter directly. A nonzero number over zero, zero over infinity, or an ordinary finite quotient must be handled by the appropriate limit reasoning instead.

\begin{bgmistake}[title={Derivative of numerator over derivative of denominator is not the quotient rule}]
The quotient rule differentiates a function \(f/g\). L'Hopital's Rule evaluates certain limits of \(f/g\). They are different theorems answering different questions.
\end{bgmistake}
\webnext{subjects/math/calculus/derivative-applications/lhopital-zero-over-zero}{L'Hopital's Rule for Zero over Zero}

\section{The Rule Has a Narrow Doorway}
\lessonobjective{Reject invalid uses and choose simpler limit methods when appropriate.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Before the formulas}]
In \emph{When Not to Use L'Hopital's Rule}, distinguish differentiating a quotient from applying L'Hopital's Rule to a quotient limit. The quotient rule finds the derivative of one function. L'Hopital compares the limit of a ratio with the limit of a ratio of derivatives under specific hypotheses. The numerator and denominator are not being canceled.

Repeated use is justified only when the new quotient remains indeterminate. Stop as soon as the limit is determined. Excess differentiation can turn a simple answer into unnecessary algebra and conceal whether the theorem was ever applicable.
\end{bgexposition}




\begin{bgexposition}[title={Use the simplest method that exposes the limit}]
Direct substitution, factoring, rationalization, dominant-term analysis, and standard limits often solve a problem with less work and more insight. L'Hopital's Rule should not replace the ability to recognize those structures.

It also cannot be used when the required hypotheses fail or when the form is not indeterminate. A finite nonzero number over zero has definite one-sided behavior, not a license to differentiate.
\end{bgexposition}

A powerful method is most useful when you know its boundaries. L'Hopital's Rule does not apply to ordinary finite quotients, does not justify differentiating numerator and denominator algebraically, and may obscure a simpler cancellation or standard limit.

On exams, state the indeterminate form before invoking the rule. That one line demonstrates that you checked the theorem's doorway instead of charging through the nearest fraction bar.

Do not use L'Hopital when:
\begin{itemize}
\item substitution gives an ordinary finite value;
\item the expression is not a quotient and has not been transformed;
\item the form is \(0/\infty\), nonzero over zero, or another determinate form;
\item differentiability hypotheses fail;
\item algebra, a standard limit, or dominant-term analysis is clearer.
\end{itemize}

\begin{bgguided}[title={Diagnose four limits}]
Decide whether L'Hopital's Rule is immediately valid.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item \(\lim_{x\to0}(x+1)/(x+2)\)
\item \(\lim_{x\to0}\sin x/x\)
\item \(\lim_{x\to\infty}x/e^x\)
\item \(\lim_{x\to0}x\ln x\)
\end{enumerate}
\begin{bgsolution}
(a) No; direct substitution gives \(1/2\). (b) Yes, form \(0/0\), though the fundamental trig limit is more foundational. (c) Yes, form \(\infty/\infty\). (d) Not immediately; transform the product into a quotient first.
\end{bgsolution}
\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgexamnote}
A valid L'Hopital solution should explicitly identify the indeterminate form. Writing ``by L'Hopital'' over a quotient with no form check is incomplete reasoning even when the final number happens to be correct.
\end{bgexamnote}
