\chapter{Modeling Studios: Derivatives in Strong Applications}
\section{How to Read a Model Critically}

A mathematical model is a deliberately simplified relationship. A derivative can analyze the model exactly while the model itself remains approximate. Good applied work therefore has two kinds of correctness: the calculus must be valid, and the model must be reasonable for the situation and range being studied.

Before trusting an answer, ask:
\begin{itemize}
\item Are the units consistent?
\item Is the requested input inside the model's meaningful domain?
\item Does the sign agree with the situation?
\item Is the magnitude plausible?
\item Which assumptions could make the conclusion unreliable?
\end{itemize}
